From 18fa33268f0b1d2becd99166012dcc84a3368232 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 21:13:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: the 3D modes of a display are not modes to
 choose from

Kodi asks the kernel for the 3D modes of the display
(DRM_CLIENT_CAP_STEREO_3D) and listed every one of them among its modes.
A 3D mode is the timing of a 2D mode sent in another layout: with it in
the list, the mode of the GUI, the adjustment of the refresh rate and the
whitelist could each land on a mode in which the display splits what it
is given into two eyes. Until the commit before this one a frame-packed
mode was also taken for a top-and-bottom one of 1080 lines (i915 with a
3D display; the other drivers list no such mode).

The 3D modes are now left out of GetModes(): what Kodi chooses from is
the 2D modes, as on a display without 3D. They stay within reach by
their index on the connector (GetStereoModes(), GetModeInfo()) for the
commits that follow, which set one only for what is drawn
stereoscopically.

What a mode says of 3D is read through DRMStereo.h, which has what is
decided and nothing of DRM or of Kodi in it: the layout as the number it
is in the flags, the lines of a frame-packed frame (1920x2205 with 45
between the eyes, 1280x1470 with 30), the choice of the output, what an
EDID says of 3D and the bytes of the vendor InfoFrame of a half layout.
The last three are used by the commits that follow.

In GetResolutionInfo() a mode without a 3D layout goes again through the
statements it went through before Dom Cobley's change; the description
of a 3D mode is his. The lines that the output plane covers are
ScanoutHeight() in both places that commit a frame (the flip and
TestFramebuffer()): vdisplay for every mode that is not frame packed.

Read and compiled (syntax check with the commands of the last build).
DRMStereo.h has a host test, drmstereo_test.cpp (152 checks, with the
address and undefined behaviour sanitizers). Not run on a display.
